 - Viewport 1280×720. `Camera2D` is a child of `Player`, zoom `2.5,2.5` (source art is 16px sprites; zoom rather than sprite scale keeps pixel-perfect rendering; the view is 512×288 world px). Default Texture Filter is **Nearest**, set project-wide.
 - **Script placement**: each `.gd` lives next to the `.tscn` it belongs to (e.g. `res://scenes/player/player.gd` beside `player.tscn`) — not centralized. `res://scripts/` is reserved for things that belong to no single scene (autoloads, shared helpers) — currently `Game` (an autoload holding the checkpoint snapshot and the respawn flow, handling `R` and owning the pause menu), and `LaserBeam` (`laser_beam.gd`, a stateless beam-solver helper used by every laser turret and every arc — see the laser bullet under World objects), and `MeltableBlock` (`meltable_block.gd`, the base class of the ice, metal and carbon fiber blocks — see the Meltable block bullet under World objects), and `SurfaceTypes` (`surface_types.gd`, static helpers that define what a tile's `surface_type` means — see the Tilemap bullet), and `LevelCatalog` (`level_catalog.gd`, the playable levels as data, in play order — see Level loading under Checkpoint and save/restore). and `TouchControls` (`touch_controls.gd`, the on-screen controls `Game` adds as a child — see Touch controls under World objects).
 - **Folder convention**: `scenes/{player,enemies,interactive,traps,levels,ui}/`, `sprites/` (subfolders per kind, `backgrounds/` for parallax art), `audio/`, `resources/` (`.tres` assets shared by more than one scene, tilesets included: a tileset used by several levels is saved here rather than embedded in each level, so every level shares the same tile data, `surface_type` custom data and collision shapes). `enemies/` = things with behavior/AI/health; `interactive/` = things the player acts on or rides (jump pads, conveyors, ladders, platforms, buttons, doors); `traps/` = things that act on the player (saw, crusher, spikes, cannon); `collectibles/` = things the player picks up.
-- **Physics layers**: layer 1 = **actors** (any `CharacterBody2D` walking under its own `move_and_slide()` — Player, Drillbot); layer 2 = **world** (solid environment an actor can stand on or be blocked/pushed by — ground tilemap, `drop_platform`, `moving_platform`, `Crusher`'s `ArmBody`, `Cannon`'s `StaticBody2D`, `RotatingBody`). New actor: always mask layer 2; mask layer 1 too only if it should be solid against other actors (default: no, to avoid enemies pushing each other around). New solid platform/hazard body: layer 2, whether it moves or not. **Exception — anything the player should be able to kick (`Mirror`, `IceBlock`, `MetalBlock`, `CarbonFiberBlock`) or bowl through with the surf (`BreakableBarrier`) sits on layers 1|2:** `KickHitbox` keeps the default mask (layer 1 only) and hits the target's physics body directly, and the bowl sweep queries layer 1 too, so a layer-2-only body is invisible to both; layer 2 keeps it solid like the rest of the world. Keep that pairing if either body's layers are ever changed. **Layer 0 with mask 2 = a body that collides with the world and that nothing else can see** (`Spark`: no query, sensor or contact filter on layer 1 or 2 finds it). **Layer 3 = the basin skim floor** (`WaterBasin`'s runtime one-way floor under a skimming surfer): only the player's mask includes it, so particles and blocks never see it. Pure-trigger `Area2D`s (gems, `Sawwheel`, `Crusher`'s root, `DrillHazard`, `KickHitbox`, `Bullet`) are unaffected — they stay on the default layer/mask unless they specifically need to detect something outside layer 1 (`Bullet` masks layers 1|2 so it also detects world tiles, not just the player). **`Area2D` never blocks movement, regardless of layer/mask** — it's a trigger only. A trap that also needs to be stood on (`Cannon`) needs a separate `StaticBody2D` on layer 2 for the solid part; the `Area2D` hazard, if any, is layered on top of that.
+- **Physics layers**: layer 1 = **actors** (any `CharacterBody2D` walking under its own `move_and_slide()` — Player, Drillbot); layer 2 = **world** (solid environment an actor can stand on or be blocked/pushed by — ground tilemap, `drop_platform`, `moving_platform`, `Crusher`'s `ArmBody`, `Cannon`'s `StaticBody2D`, `RotatingBody`). New actor: always mask layer 2; mask layer 1 too only if it should be solid against other actors (default: no, to avoid enemies pushing each other around). New solid platform/hazard body: layer 2, whether it moves or not. **Exception — anything the player should be able to kick (`Mirror`, `IceBlock`, `MetalBlock`, `CarbonFiberBlock`) or bowl through with the surf (`BreakableBarrier`) sits on layers 1|2:** `KickHitbox` keeps the default mask (layer 1 only) and hits the target's physics body directly, and the bowl sweep queries layer 1 too, so a layer-2-only body is invisible to both; layer 2 keeps it solid like the rest of the world. Keep that pairing if either body's layers are ever changed. **Layer 0 with mask 2 = a body that collides with the world and that nothing else can see** (`Spark`: no query, sensor or contact filter on layer 1 or 2 finds it). **Layer 4 = beam targets** (the `BeamTarget` `Area2D` each receiver owns, in the group of its kind, `beam_target_laser` / `_arc` / `_freeze`: a beam solved with a `target_kind` casts one extra ray against layer 4 only and accepts only its own kind, so no other trigger can stop a beam and a laser is not drawn into a freeze receiver; nothing else may sit on layer 4). **Layer 3 = the basin skim floor** (`WaterBasin`'s runtime one-way floor under a skimming surfer): only the player's mask includes it, so particles and blocks never see it. Pure-trigger `Area2D`s (gems, `Sawwheel`, `Crusher`'s root, `DrillHazard`, `KickHitbox`, `Bullet`) are unaffected — they stay on the default layer/mask unless they specifically need to detect something outside layer 1 (`Bullet` masks layers 1|2 so it also det